    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The slope of the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is $- \\frac {2}{3}$.\nTherefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to it is $\\boxed{\\frac {3}{2}}$.\n\nTo find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we start by determining the slope of the given line. The equation of the line is in the standard form \\(Ax + By = C\\). To find the slope, we can rewrite the equation in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n\nStarting with the given equation:\n\\[2x + 3y = 6\\]\n\nWe isolate \\(y\\) by first subtracting \\(2x\\) from both sides:\n\\[3y = -2x + 6\\]\n\nNext, we divide every term by 3:\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can see that the slope \\(m\\) of the line is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\nThe slope of a line that is perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the given line is:\n\\[-\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
